  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSaturday/Saturday Night...\n\nWeak surface boundary is likely to be in the vicinity of the KY/TN \nborder region Saturday morning and will likely drift northward \nslightly during the day.  Aloft, another upper level shortwave axis \nwill pivot through the region and likely result in another round of \nshowers and storms across the region.   Model soundings show \ntall/skinny CAPE profiles with weak shear and a high PWAT airmass in \nplace.  So slow moving showers/storms with heavy rainfall and gusty \nwinds looks likely for Saturday.  Highs on the day should top out in \nthe 82-87 degree range with overnight lows in the upper 60s to \naround 70.\n\nSunday through Thursday...\n\nBy Sunday, upper trough axis is forecast to shift to our east. \nStrong mid-level ridging will build into the region from the west \nand a period of dry weather with above normal temperatures are \nexpected.  Highs of 85-90 are expected for Sunday and Monday before \nwarming into the 87-92 degree range by Tuesday and Wednesday. \nEarlier 10/00Z guidance suite suggested the ridge breaking down by \nmid-week.  However, latest Euro Op/Ensembles are a bit slower with \nthis potential breakdown holding on to the ridge into Thursday.  If \nthe ridge were to break down and we get a front into the region, \nhighs would back off into the upper 80s.  However, a slower solution \nwould result in a delay and highs on Thursday could remain in the \nlower 90s.\n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nThe month of September is off to a hot start.  Thus far we're \nrunning about +8.5F above normal at SDF, +8.6F at BWG, and +7.6F at \nLEX.  We're likely to keep those above average temperatures going \ndue to the expected warm period of weather for much of next week.\n\nLooking at the hemispheric scale, we're starting to see some \ninteresting changes on the horizon.  Quite a few of the extended \nmodels and ensembles are indicating a cool down for our area as we \nenter late September, probably after the 20th or so.  The forcing \nmechanism behind that appears to be a combination of factors that \nmay come together.  The Euro and its ensembles have been fairly \nintent on developing a strong area of high pressure over western \nAsia just north of the Himalayas.  If this occurs, we'll get a \nstrong pressure gradient there which will result in a good amount of \nmomentum transfer into the atmosphere.  Global atmospheric angular \nmomentum charts really ramp up over the next 2 weeks and this should \nlead to a Asian jet enhancement and eventually a Pacific jet \nextension and enhancement.  The downstream affects here would lead \nto a stronger ridge over AK and a +PNA pattern.  Downstream, decent \ntroughing looks to develop across the eastern CONUS.  Interestingly \nenough, the west Pacific looks to be favorable for continued typhoon \nactivity and we'll be watching for any typhoons that recurve and \nmerge into the Pacific Jet which could yield stronger impacts to the \nflow over the CONUS later this month. Considerable uncertainty \nremains as to how much the pattern will flex, but confidence is \nincreasing that seasonal fall air will be possible across our area \nfor late September into October.",
